Task 1: Changing Process Priority Using nice and renice
Subtask 1.1: Launch a Test Process
Open a terminal.
Run a CPU-intensive command (e.g., sha1sum /dev/zero) in the background:
sha1sum /dev/zero &
Expected Output: The process starts, and its PID is displayed.
Subtask 1.2: Check Default Priority
Use ps to check the nice value (NI) of the process:
ps -l -p $(pgrep sha1sum)
Expected Output: The NI column shows 0 (default priority).
Subtask 1.3: Adjust Priority Using nice
Launch a new instance with a modified priority (-n sets nice value):

nice -n 10 sha1sum /dev/zero &
Explanation: A higher nice value (10) lowers priority.
Verify the new nice value:

ps -l -p $(pgrep sha1sum | tail -1)
Expected Output: The new process shows NI as 10.
Subtask 1.4: Adjust Priority of Running Process Using renice
Change the priority of an existing process (replace PID):

sudo renice -n 5 -p $(pgrep sha1sum | head -1)
Explanation: -n 5 sets a higher priority than 10 but lower than 0.
Confirm the change:

ps -l -p $(pgrep sha1sum | head -1)
Expected Output: The NI column updates to 5.
Task 2: Monitoring Processes with top and htop
Subtask 2.1: Use top to View Process Stats
Run top:

top
Key Observations:
PR (Priority) and NI (Nice) columns.
CPU usage per process.
Press q to exit.

Subtask 2.2: Install and Use htop (If Not Installed)
Install htop:

sudo apt install htop       # Debian/Ubuntu  
sudo dnf install htop       # RHEL/CentOS/Fedora  
Launch htop:

htop
Key Features:
Color-coded CPU/memory usage.
Tree view of processes.
Interactive priority adjustment (F7/F8).
Press F10 to exit.

Task 3: Understanding Process States and Dynamic Adjustments
Subtask 3.1: Identify Process States
List processes with states (R=Running, S=Sleeping, D=Uninterruptible):
ps aux
Subtask 3.2: Kill a Low-Priority Process
Terminate the sha1sum process:
kill $(pgrep sha1sum)
Subtask 3.3: Adjust Scheduling Policy (Optional)
Use chrt to set a real-time policy (requires sudo):
sudo chrt -f -p 99 $(pgrep sha1sum)
Explanation: -f sets FIFO scheduling (priority 99).
